# Cloudflare Pages headers.
#
# ─── DELIBERATELY ABSENT: Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy ────────────────────────
# COEP + COOP would enable SharedArrayBuffer and multi-threaded WebAssembly, and
# it is tempting for an image-processing site. Do not add them here:
#
#   1. The jSquash codecs used on this site are single-threaded builds and do
#      not need SharedArrayBuffer.
#   2. Cross-origin isolation blocks third-party iframes that do not opt in,
#      which breaks Google AdSense completely.
#
# ─── DELIBERATELY ABSENT: Content-Security-Policy ─────────────────────────────
# AdSense loads from a wide and changing set of Google domains. A CSP that is
# even slightly wrong fails silently and stops ads rendering, with no error that
# points at the cause. Add one only after ads are live and verified, and test it
# on a preview deployment first.

# Hashed build assets never change content, so cache them forever. Astro emits
# every JS bundle and every WASM codec here with a content hash in the filename,
# so this one rule covers all of them.
#
# There used to be separate `/*.wasm` and `/*.js` rules as well. Both matched the
# same files as this rule, and Cloudflare Pages CONCATENATES matching rules, so
# the live response carried a doubled header:
#   Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable, public, max-age=…
# Harmless, but `/*.js` was also a trap: it matches any path depth, so any
# non-hashed script added at the root would silently become uncacheable-away for
# a year. If a JS or WASM file ever ships outside /_astro/, give it its own rule
# rather than reintroducing a global glob.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Icons change rarely but are not content-hashed, so cache for a day.
/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/apple-touch-icon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  # This site never needs any of these. Denying them is a clear signal that a
  # tool asking you to drop private photos onto it is not reaching for hardware.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
